//! **The DERIVED deref, as a frame-generic activation lemma.**
//!
//! `ir_lzb_deref_activation` is seven steps of an AUTHORED model. This is the
//! same fact for the four bodies the compiler actually emits —
//! `<LevelArc as Deref>::deref`, `Option::as_deref`, `Option::expect` and
//! `<Arc<Level>>::deref` — and it is forty.
//!
//! ## Why every intermediate machine here is a REDUCTION, never a transcription
//!
//! The proof has to expose four heap operations syntactically so a
//! representation hypothesis can rewrite them: the `Option<Arc<Level>>` load at
//! the field cell, the `Arc` load at the same cell one frame deeper, and the
//! return slot's store and load. Each needs the machine state at that point,
//! and those states are three-frame stacks with their own bindings — exactly
//! the kind of term that is wrong by one binding and fails an hour later.
//!
//! [`ir_cfg_mach`] removes the question: a machine is named as
//! `ir_cfg_mach (ir_steps k M c) d`, which the kernel REDUCES to the machine
//! the run is actually in. Nothing is copied, so nothing can be copied wrong.
//!
//! `DerivedProved`, empty axiom closures.

use crate::spec::error::SpecError;
use crate::spec::Specification;

